From e481f64f1ff7da36e4a0bded1852b5fb4e984532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:05:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Post-processings computed by kernels
 (MFEM_MGIS_POST_PROCESSINGS_COMPUTATION)

With MFEM_MGIS_POST_PROCESSINGS_COMPUTATION=Kernels (ElementByElement by
default), the computations on functions defined at the integration
points used by the post-processings are made by kernels, on the device
if a device is enabled, instead of loops over the elements evaluating
their transformations point by point:

- computeMeasure and computeIntegral, for the behaviour integrators
  supporting kernels (weights w det J): reduction over the elements;
- computeL2Projection and the implicit gradient regularization: right-hand
  sides by a kernel, summed by the element restriction of MFEM; operator
  assembled by MFEM on the device (AssemblyLevel::FULL) for the elements
  with a tensor basis, element by element otherwise;
- updateGridFunction (values at the nodes, arithmetic mean of the local
  projections): local projections by a kernel (exact mass matrices,
  Cholesky factorization), sums and counts by the element restriction,
  then over the processes by the prolongation, the choice being made by
  all the processes before any exchange.

The kernels are declared in MFEMMGIS/Batch/IntegrationPointsFunctions.hxx.
The other cases (functions on submeshes or boundaries, integration rules
differing between elements, non conforming meshes, elements of different
geometries) keep the computations element by element. The counter
getNumberOfPostProcessingsComputationsByKernels makes the choice
observable.

The L2 projection keeps the operator handle given by FormLinearSystem,
which owns the matrix built by the full assembly (a reference to it was
kept beyond its lifetime).

Test: PostProcessingsKernelsTest compares both computations (measures,
integrals, values at the nodes, L2 projections of a scalar and of a
vector function) on hexahedra with two materials, tetrahedra and
quadrangles, on the host, on the debug device, on two processes and on
the GPU.
